From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] v8: fix TimeTicks::Now() mach timebase overflow on ppc
 darwin

Same bug class as the libuv uv__hrtime() overflow (patch 0026), in
V8's copy of the mach timebase scaling. TimeTicks::Now() computes
mach_absolute_time() / 1000 * numer / denom; the division by 1000
happens first, so on PowerPC (numer/denom ~= 1000000000/33333333) the
intermediate product overflows 2^64 only after ~6.4 days of host
uptime instead of ~9 minutes - but a desktop G5 is easily up that
long, and past that point V8's monotonic clock becomes a sawtooth.
TimeTicks feeds GC heuristics, the compiler dispatcher, and profiling
deadlines, so a wrap shows up as erratic pauses rather than a clean
abort.

Apply the same split scaling: (t / denom) * numer cannot overflow for
centuries, and (t % denom) * numer stays below ~3.4e16. ThreadTicks on
Darwin needs no change (thread_info already reports microseconds).
---
 deps/v8/src/base/platform/time.cc | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/deps/v8/src/base/platform/time.cc b/deps/v8/src/base/platform/time.cc
index f07fd8e5..08e0739d 100644
--- a/deps/v8/src/base/platform/time.cc
+++ b/deps/v8/src/base/platform/time.cc
@@ -723,8 +723,15 @@ TimeTicks TimeTicks::Now() {
     DCHECK_EQ(KERN_SUCCESS, result);
     USE(result);
   }
-  ticks = (mach_absolute_time() / Time::kNanosecondsPerMicrosecond *
-           info.numer / info.denom);
+  // On PowerPC the timebase ticks at tens of MHz, so numer/denom is
+  // ~1000000000/33333333 rather than 1/1: multiplying the raw tick count by
+  // numer overflows uint64_t within minutes of host uptime, and dividing by
+  // 1000 first only defers the wrap to a few days. Split the scaling so no
+  // intermediate value can exceed 2^64.
+  uint64_t t = mach_absolute_time();
+  ticks = ((t / info.denom) * info.numer +
+           (t % info.denom) * info.numer / info.denom) /
+          Time::kNanosecondsPerMicrosecond;
 #elif V8_OS_SOLARIS
   ticks = (gethrtime() / Time::kNanosecondsPerMicrosecond);
 #elif V8_OS_POSIX
